<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Novosibirsk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Novonikolayevsk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Novosibirsk is the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the third-largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on the Ob River. It was founded in 1893 where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> bridged the Ob, and grew rapidly as a railway, industrial and administrative centre. Its scientific town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akademgorodok">Akademgorodok</a> made it one of the main centres of Soviet research.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/novosibirsk.jpg" alt="Novosibirsk" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boris Sakic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opera-and-Ballet-theatre-Novosibirsk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Novosibirsk stands on the banks of the Ob River in the West Siberian Plain, in the southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, about 2,811 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Its southern boundary runs through the Novosibirsk reservoir, formed by the dam of the Novosibirsk Hydroelectric Station, which cuts through the Ob Plateau. The nearest major cities are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omsk">Omsk</a>, Barnaul, Kemerovo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a>. The climate is typical of Siberia, with dry winters and temperatures far below freezing, since there is no ocean nearby and no tall mountains to the north to hold back the Arctic winds. It is the second-farthest substantially populated city from the ocean, after Ürümqi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Russian town began on 30 April 1893 on the right bank of the Ob, near an area traditionally inhabited by the Chat Tatars, at the future site of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> bridge. In 1895 it became known as Novonikolayevsk, in honour both of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a> and of the reigning tsar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>. It superseded the village of Bolshoye Krivoshchyokovo on the opposite bank, founded in 1696, which was resettled in 1893 because of the bridge. The bridge was completed in the spring of 1897 and made the settlement a regional transport hub. Its importance grew with the completion of the Turkestan-Siberia Railway in the early 20th century, which linked it directly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>.</p><p>Before 1917 Novonikolayevsk had no traditional centre: the railway management buildings, the station, the chief cathedral and the city government were spread across the town. After the revolution Lenin House was built in 1925 on Krasny Prospekt, and the first Lenin monument stood near the station. The city was ravaged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> but recovered in the early Soviet period and took its present name, Novosibirsk, in 1926. Work on the Opera House began in 1931 on the former market square, and under Stalin the city became one of the largest industrial centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. After the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it received many factories moved from the Russian core.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Novosibirsk is the administrative centre of Novosibirsk Oblast and the Siberian Federal District, and the third most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with 1,633,595 people at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2021. Its landmarks include the neo-Byzantine Alexander Nevsky Cathedral and the Novosibirsk Opera and Ballet Theatre, and Tolmachevo Airport is the busiest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. The metro, opened in 1985, was the first in Siberia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Novosibirsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
